    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nDealer Trying To Sell 1970 Pontiac Trans Am With Only 36,000 Miles, Too Bad for the Upgrades\n27 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller Consignify1\nWhat a car. This 1970 Trans Am looks almost too good to be true, but unfortunately, the changes that it received, including the custom interior, are likely to make some people walk away.\nThe Trans Am wasn’t yet a thing in 1970. This was only the second year for the new Pontiac Firebird, but this specimen is here to demonstrate that the low production numbers didn’t mean the car wasn’t a desirable configuration.\nIn fact, the low production numbers make 1970 Trans Ams even more desirable today, and that’s another reason why stepping away from the day-one glory doesn’t make sense. However, this car is still an intriguing presence on the road, and I don’t think it’ll take long before it sells.\nPhoto: eBay seller Consignify1 Pontiac produced 48,739 Firebirds for the 1970 model year, and the Esprit was the most popular configuration, accounting for 18,961 orders. The base Firebird was the runner-up with 18,.874 units, and these numbers make perfect sense. These were the most affordable Firebird versions, and considering the 1970 model year came with new regulations and an increased focus on efficient engines, paying less for a new car was on the agenda for many Americans.\nAs a result, six-cylinder engines continued to be preferred over V8 configurations. In the base Firebird family, the six represented over 3,100 orders.\nThe Formula was third in buyers’ preferences, accounting for only 7,708 units. Only 3,196 Trans Ams ended up rolling off the assembly lines.\nThe Trans Am was standard with the L74 400 Ram Air III, and Pontiac offered two transmission options. Most people picked the manual, while 1,339 buyers went for an automatic.\nFew people found the dealer-based method to get the optional Ram Air IV, so if you somehow come across a Trans Am with this engine under the hood, you’d better not miss the opportunity to buy it. Only 88 such cars were ordered, and 66 came with a manual transmission.\nPhoto: eBay seller Consignify1 The first thing I want to highlight about this 1970 Trans Am is the mileage on the clock.\nThe dealer claims the 36,000 miles you’ll notice when you look at the odometer is the actual mileage, so this Trans Am never spent much time on the road. It’s a garage-kept and well-maintained specimen, according to the dealer, which kind of explains its condition today, especially if no major repairs were involved.\nI would still call the dealer and arrange a live inspection because there’s only one way to make sure that everything is as advertised. However, I would also ask questions about the previous ownership and the work this Trans Am has received, as I wouldn’t be surprised to learn that the paint is no longer original and the undersides have already been redone.\nWhat we do know is that the cabin has been “tastefully upgraded,” as the dealer explains. The car is, therefore, sporting a custom black interior. I admit that it looks good, cozy, comfortable, you name it, but considering the low production numbers, I would have preferred a 1970 Trans Am with the original cabin.\nThis could make it harder for the car to find a new home, but eventually, someone will probably take it home, especially considering the engine under the hood.\nIt’s unclear if this is the original setup, but the Trans Am is fitted with a 400ci engine and an automatic transmission. No further specifics were shared, but the vehicle should be able to run and drive properly, meaning that taking it home doesn’t necessarily involve a trailer.\nPhoto: eBay seller Consignify1 Based on the photos shared on eBay, the Trans Am doesn’t require further investments, so it’s ready to be driven and admired at car shows. On the other hand, without all the big questions getting answers, I think that the only way to make sure you buy the right Trans Am is to call the dealer and go there in person.\nBefore you do this, you must convince your significant other that paying almost $50K for this car is worth it. The dealer wants $48,000, but considering the Make Offer option has also been enabled, you might be able to get a small discount if you’re committed to a purchase.\nThe listing will stay up for a few more weeks, and if you want to see the car in person, you’ll have to get a ticket to Naples, Florida. You should be able to avoid shipping costs, considering the car’s mechanical condition, obviously if you don’t mind the road trip back home.\nWe’re not being told anything about the papers, but a clean title should also be available. A 1970 Trans Am would be quite a museum piece if any original goodies are still around, including the build sheet and maybe other documents, so make sure you ask these questions when you go see the car with your own eyes.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTrans Am pontiac trans am 1970 Trans Am Pontiac WEEKEND\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT Widebody Mercedes-AMG C 63 Cabrio Is the Perfect CLE Antidote",
